<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Antalcidas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">387–386 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as King's Peace · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of Antalcidas, or King's Peace, was the settlement imposed by the Persian king Artaxerxes II in 387–386 BCE, negotiated by the Spartan Antalcidas, that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a>. It recognised Persian rule over the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and declared all other Greek cities autonomous. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> used the autonomy clause to break up rival leagues such as the Boeotian League.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-antalcidas.jpg" alt="Peace of Antalcidas" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carleton L. (Carleton Lewis), (1866-1948) Publication date 1918-1922 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King%27s_Peace_387_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was more commonly known in antiquity as the King's Peace, a name that reflects the depth of Persian influence, as Persian gold had driven the preceding war. It was a form of Common Peace, like the Thirty Years' Peace that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-peloponnesian-war">First Peloponnesian War</a>. By 387 BCE the central front of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a> had shifted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, where an Athenian fleet under Thrasybulus had placed a number of cities under Athenian control and was acting with Evagoras, the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Evagoras was an enemy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and many of the Athenian gains threatened Persian interests, so Artaxerxes II switched his support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and its allies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>.</p><p>Antalcidas, commander of a Spartan fleet, was summoned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> along with the satrap Tiribazus, and there the Spartans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> worked out an agreement. To bring the Athenians to the table, Antalcidas moved 90 ships to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>, where he could threaten the grain route from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. The Athenians remembered their defeat in 404 BCE, when the Spartans had controlled the Hellespont, and agreed to negotiate. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>, Corinth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>, unwilling to fight on without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, were also forced to negotiate, and at a conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> all the belligerents accepted the terms laid down by Artaxerxes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> records the decree, Artaxerxes held it just that the cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> should belong to him, as well as Clazomenae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> among the islands, and that the other Greek cities should be autonomous, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbros">Imbros</a> and Scyros, which should belong to the Athenians. Ionia and Cyprus were abandoned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, and the Athenians had to give up their gains in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. The insistence on autonomy ended the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and Corinth, which had merged politically in a sympoliteia with joint citizenship, and the Thebans had to disband their Boeotian league. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a> was overlooked.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> regained firm control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> and parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, and the treaty placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> under Persian suzerainty. Will Durant called it the most disgraceful event in Greek history. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, charged with enforcing the peace, forced Mantinea to disband into villages and in 382 BCE campaigned against the Chalcidian League. In 383 its commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoebidas">Phoebidas</a> seized the Theban Kadmeia. Pelopidas and his companions liberated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> in 379 BCE, and general fighting resumed in the Boeotian War of 378 to 371 BCE. The idea of a Common Peace endured, but later attempts had little more success.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peace of Antalcidas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Miletus revolted from Athens in 412 BCE and became a Spartan base, and under the <b>King's Peace</b> of 387/386 BCE it returned to Persian rule, later under the Hecatomnid satraps of Caria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>King's Peace</b> of 387/386 BCE restored Spartan dominance on the mainland under Agesilaus II, but the seizure of the Theban citadel in 382 BCE provoked a war that ended in disaster.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>King's Peace</b> of 387 BCE named Clazomenae as Persian territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/clazomenae">Clazomenae</a></li><li><blockquote>Spartan high-handedness after the <b>King's Peace</b> of 386 BCE, and a raid on Attica by the Spartan Sphodrias, pushed Athens to found a new alliance in 378-377 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-athenian-league">Second Athenian League</a></li></ul></section>
